## 4. Deep Dive Sections

### 📈 Series

**What it is:** A unique metric name + label set combination.
**What it contains:** A stream of `(timestamp, value)` samples over time.
**Why it matters:** It's the fundamental unit chunks are scoped to — everything else builds on "which series does this sample belong to."

```
Example A                          Example B
┌────────────────────────┐         ┌────────────────────────┐
│ 📈 http_requests_total   │         │ 📈 http_requests_total   │
│ {method="GET"}          │         │ {method="POST"}         │
└────────────────────────┘         └────────────────────────┘
```
Same metric name, different label (`GET` vs `POST`) → **two distinct series**, each getting its own separate chunks.

---

### 📦 Chunk

**What it is:** A compressed sequence of samples for a **single time series**, covering a time range.
**What it contains:** Up to 120 samples (default) using XOR/Gorilla-style compression (delta-of-delta timestamps, XOR'd values), reaching ~1–2 bytes/sample.
**Why it matters:** This is the actual compressed data payload — one chunk per series per roughly 2-hour window at typical scrape intervals.

```
Example A (120 samples)            Example B (fewer samples, shorter window)
┌────────────────────────┐         ┌────────────────────────┐
│ 📦 CHUNK                 │         │ 📦 CHUNK                 │
│ series: cpu_usage        │         │ series: cpu_usage        │
│ 120 samples, ~2h window  │         │ 40 samples, <1h window   │
│ XOR-compressed           │         │ XOR-compressed           │
└────────────────────────┘         └────────────────────────┘
```
Same series, different sample counts → chunk size/duration varies with scrape frequency and how full the chunk got before rollover, but the compression scheme and per-series scoping stay the same.

---

### 🗄️ Block

**What it is:** A directory on disk holding **all series' data for a fixed time range** (default 2 hours when freshly flushed).
**What it contains:** A `chunks/` folder (many series' chunk files packed together), an `index` (series/labels → chunk locations), `tombstones` (deletion records), and `meta.json` (min/max time, stats, compaction info).
**Why it matters:** It's the umbrella structure and the unit compaction operates on — the reverse containment direction from Loki's chunk.

```
Example A (fresh block)            Example B (compacted block)
┌────────────────────────┐         ┌────────────────────────┐
│ 🗄️ BLOCK                 │         │ 🗄️ BLOCK                 │
│ 2h window                │         │ 36h window (merged)      │
│ chunks/ + index + meta   │         │ chunks/ + index + meta   │
│ from ONE flush            │        │ merged from MANY blocks  │
└────────────────────────┘         └────────────────────────┘
```
A freshly-flushed block covers a short window (2h); after compaction, a block covers a much larger merged window (up to ~10 days) while still keeping the same internal shape (`chunks/`, `index`, `tombstones`, `meta.json`).

---

### 🧹 Compaction

**What it is:** The background process that merges multiple smaller blocks into fewer, larger blocks.
**What it contains (as steps):** Merge same-series chunks → rebuild index → apply tombstones (real deletion) → remove old blocks.
**Why it matters:** Keeps per-block index overhead down and lets multi-day queries open far fewer blocks. Step pattern: **2h → 36h → up to ~10 days** (exact ratios depend on version/config).

---

## 5. Full Lifecycle Diagram 🔁

```
1️⃣ Sample scraped for a series (metric + labels, ts + value)
        ▼
2️⃣ 📦 Samples accumulate into a per-series CHUNK
      (up to 120 samples, XOR/Gorilla compressed)
        ▼
3️⃣ 🧠 Chunk lives in the in-memory HEAD BLOCK
      (all active series, most recent ~2h)
        ▼
4️⃣ 🗄️ Head block flushes to disk as a BLOCK directory
      (chunks/ + index + tombstones + meta.json)
        ▼
5️⃣ Repeat 1️⃣–4️⃣ → many small 2h blocks accumulate
        ▼
6️⃣ 🧹 COMPACTOR merges same-series chunks across blocks,
      rebuilds index, applies tombstones, deletes old blocks
        ▼
7️⃣ 🗄️ Fewer, larger BLOCKS remain (36h → up to ~10 days)
```

---

## 6. Comparison Example ⚖️

| Scenario | Loki | Prometheus |
|---|---|---|
| **Chunk** | Outer container: multiple blocks + labels for one stream | Inner unit: one series' compressed samples |
| **Block** | Inner unit: a batch of log lines inside a chunk | Outer container: all series' chunks + index for a time range |
| **Compaction** | Merges small chunk index files, dedupes | Merges multiple time-range blocks into fewer, larger ones; also drops deleted data |

**Cause → effect:** The word "chunk" and "block" are reused in both systems, but the containment relationship **inverts**: Loki chunk ⊃ blocks, while Prometheus block ⊃ chunks. Reading Prometheus docs with a Loki mental model (or vice versa) will get the hierarchy backwards.

