## 8. Key Takeaways ✅

**Containment order**
- ✅ Samples go **into** a chunk (one series per chunk).
- ✅ Chunks go **into** a block (many series per block).
- ✅ The **block** is the outer/umbrella structure — the opposite of Loki, where the chunk is the umbrella.

**Compression & efficiency**
- ✅ Chunks use **XOR/Gorilla-style compression** — delta-of-delta timestamps + XOR'd values — getting down to ~1–2 bytes/sample.
- ✅ Default cap: **120 samples per chunk**, roughly matching a ~2-hour window at typical scrape intervals.

**Lifecycle & compaction**
- ✅ New data lives in the in-memory **head block** first, then flushes to a disk **block** (default 2h).
- ✅ **Compaction** merges small blocks into larger ones (2h → 36h → up to ~10 days), rebuilding the index and actually applying tombstoned deletions.

**One-line summary:**
📈 Sample → 📦 Chunk (per series, compressed) → 🧠 Head block (in-memory) → 🗄️ Block (on-disk, all series + index) → 🧹 Compaction (merges blocks over time)

