### Plain-language recap

The source explained that Prometheus reuses the words "chunk" and "block" from Loki but with the containment **inverted**. In Prometheus, a **chunk** is the compressed data for a single time series (up to 120 samples, XOR/Gorilla-compressed, ~1–2 bytes/sample) covering roughly a 2-hour window. A **block** is the umbrella: an on-disk directory holding every series' chunks for that time range, plus an index, tombstones, and metadata. New data first lives in an in-memory **head block** before being flushed to disk as a persisted block. Over time, the **compactor** merges many small blocks into fewer, larger ones (following a step pattern like 2h → 36h → up to ~10 days), rebuilding the index and permanently applying any deletions along the way. The key contrast with Loki: Loki's chunk contains blocks, while Prometheus's block contains chunks — same terms, opposite direction.
